<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Saltukids</h1><p class="st-meta">1072 CE – 1202 CE · capital Erzurum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the first Turkish principalities in eastern Anatolia, ruling Erzurum and fighting Georgia for over a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition holds that the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> granted the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> to the commander Saltuk after the victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in 1071. His descendants established a hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> there, among the earliest Turkish states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Saltukids held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tercan">Tercan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayburt">Bayburt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ispir">Ispir</a>, on the frontier with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia</a>, which defeated them several times; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-din-saltuk-ii">Izz al-Din Saltuk II</a> was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> in the 1150s and ransomed. Erzurum prospered from the trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. Surviving monuments include the tomb tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Saltuk and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepsi-minaret">Tepsi Minaret</a> in Erzurum.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1202 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Rum Seljuk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleymanshah-ii">Suleymanshah II</a>, campaigning against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a>, annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> and gave it to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughith-al-din-tughrilshah">Tughrilshah</a>, ending the dynasty.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/saltukids.jpg" alt="Saltukids" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: Armenica.org · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shaddadid.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1072 CE – 1202 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Erzurum</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Izz al-Din Saltuk II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Saltukid dynasty, Beylik of Erzurum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1137&amp;focus=saltukids" aria-label="Saltukids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5697 750 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Saltukids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6017 1006L6481 1127M6017 1006L5810 1072"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6481 1127h0M5810 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6017 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6017 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Erzurum. <a href="/atlas?year=1137&amp;focus=saltukids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

